Subject: [PATCH] A window's shaft fades over its own fall to the floor

Patch 0052 of the renderer series (OoT: Recompiled).

The user, 2026-10-09, of the Master Sword room's tall window: the shaft should start "at window
and fade closer to the floor". 0051 faded every shaft over a fixed distance in world units from the
glass, which suited the hall's windows and left the tall window's shaft, whose glass stands about
twice as high, faded to almost nothing where it can be seen.

Each window's light is now followed from its glass to the first surface it falls on
(shaftLanding: a shadow ray answers whether anything stands in a stretch and where one thing does,
not the nearest, so the stretch is halved six times, each answer a nearer bound). Every pixel asks
about the same ray, so the answer is the same everywhere; only a pixel whose own ray crosses the
beam asks. The Shaft length (tuneShafts.y) is now the share of that fall over which the shaft fades
to a third, clamped to 0.05 to 4, and the march ends where the light lands. The program's default
moves from 600 units to 0.45 of the fall.

diff --git a/src/shaders/Raytracing.hlsl b/src/shaders/Raytracing.hlsl
--- a/src/shaders/Raytracing.hlsl
+++ b/src/shaders/Raytracing.hlsl
@@ -1862,10 +1862,9 @@ float3 dustInLight(float3 origin, float3 direction, float end, uint2 launchIndex
 // through the air below it. The light is the window's own color and strength.
 //
 // The Window shafts row (tuneShafts.x, 0 off) scales the air's brightness per world unit of beam
-// crossed, and the Shaft length row (tuneShafts.y) is the distance from the glass over which the
-// shaft fades to about a third. Nothing here touches the light on surfaces.
+// crossed, and the Shaft length row (tuneShafts.y) is the share of the window's own fall over
+// which the shaft fades to about a third (patch 0052). Nothing here touches the light on surfaces.
 static const uint ShaftSteps = 12;
-static const float ShaftFadeReach = 5.0f;   // past this many fade lengths a shaft is not marched at all
 
 // Narrows [t0, t1] to where a + b t lies within [lo, hi].
 void clipShaftSlab(float a, float b, float lo, float hi, inout float t0, inout float t1) {
@@ -1882,13 +1881,50 @@ void clipShaftSlab(float a, float b, float lo, float hi, inout float t0, inout f
     t1 = min(t1, max(ta, tb));
 }
 
+// THE SHAFT FADES OVER ITS OWN FALL (patch 0052, the user, 2026-10-09, of the Master Sword room's
+// tall window: the shaft "starts at window and fades closer to the floor"). A fade measured in
+// world units from the glass suited the hall's windows and left the tall window's shaft, whose
+// glass stands twice as high, faded to almost nothing where it can be seen. So each window's
+// light is followed from its glass to the first thing it falls on, and the Shaft length row is a
+// share of that fall: the shaft is at full strength at the glass and fades on the way down,
+// whatever the height of the window.
+//
+// The first surface is found by halving, because a shadow ray answers whether anything stands in a
+// stretch and where ONE thing does, not the nearest: each answer is a nearer bound. Every pixel asks
+// about the same ray from the same glass, so the answer is the same everywhere and the rays travel
+// together; it is asked only by a pixel whose own ray crosses the beam.
+static const uint ShaftLandingHalvings = 6;
+
+float shaftLanding(PointLight light, float3 falls, float reach) {
+    const float3 start = light.position + falls * WindowClearance;
+    float hitDistance;
+    if (traceVisibilityRange(start, falls, 0.0f, reach, hitDistance) > 0.5f) {
+        return reach;
+    }
+
+    float near = 0.0f;
+    float far = max(hitDistance, 1.0f);
+    for (uint h = 0; h < ShaftLandingHalvings; h++) {
+        const float middle = 0.5f * (near + far);
+        if (traceVisibilityRange(start, falls, 0.0f, middle, hitDistance) > 0.5f) {
+            near = middle;
+        }
+        else {
+            far = max(hitDistance, 1.0f);
+        }
+    }
+
+    return WindowClearance + far;
+}
+
 float3 windowShafts(float3 origin, float3 direction, float end, float jitter) {
     const float strength = RtParams.tuneShafts.x;
     if (strength <= 0.0f) {
         return float3(0.0f, 0.0f, 0.0f);
     }
 
-    const float shaftLength = max(RtParams.tuneShafts.y, 50.0f);
+    // The Shaft length row as a share of each window's own fall (patch 0052).
+    const float shaftShare = clamp(RtParams.tuneShafts.y, 0.05f, 4.0f);
     const uint count = min(RtParams.lightsCount, RtParams.maxLights);
     float3 sum = 0.0f;
     for (uint i = 0; i < count; i++) {
@@ -1912,7 +1948,9 @@ float3 windowShafts(float3 origin, float3 direction, float end, float jitter) {
         const float i1 = dot(direction, inward);
         const float3 onGlassA = rel - falls * (i0 / approach);
         const float3 onGlassB = direction - falls * (i1 / approach);
-        const float reach = ShaftFadeReach * shaftLength;
+        // As far as the window's light reaches at all, first; then, for a pixel whose ray crosses
+        // that, as far as it falls to the first surface (patch 0052).
+        const float reach = max(light.attenuationExponent, 100.0f);
         const float margin = 2.0f + WindowEdge * reach;
         float t0 = 0.0f;
         float t1 = end;
@@ -1923,6 +1961,13 @@ float3 windowShafts(float3 origin, float3 direction, float end, float jitter) {
             continue;
         }
 
+        const float landing = shaftLanding(light, falls, reach);
+        clipShaftSlab(i0, i1, WindowGlassDepth, (landing + WindowEdge * landing + 2.0f) * approach, t0, t1);
+        if (t1 <= t0) {
+            continue;
+        }
+
+        const float shaftLength = max(shaftShare * landing, 20.0f);
         const float stepLength = (t1 - t0) / float(ShaftSteps);
         float lit = 0.0f;
         for (uint s = 0; s < ShaftSteps; s++) {
